WEBVTT

00:00.535 --> 00:02.301
- [Instructor] So we're
told that this table

00:02.301 --> 00:05.837
gives select values of the
differentiable function F.

00:05.837 --> 00:07.929
So it gives us the value of the function

00:07.929 --> 00:11.082
at a few values for X, in particular,

00:11.082 --> 00:12.950
five different values for X and

00:12.950 --> 00:15.813
it tells us what the
corresponding f(x) is.

00:15.813 --> 00:17.921
And they say, what is the best estimate

00:17.921 --> 00:19.762
for f'(4)?

00:19.762 --> 00:22.158
So this is the derivative
of our function F

00:22.158 --> 00:24.094
when X is equal to four.

00:24.094 --> 00:25.809
Or another way to think about it,

00:25.809 --> 00:27.402
what is the slope of the tangent line

00:27.402 --> 00:29.960
when X is equal to four for f(x)?

00:29.960 --> 00:32.454
So what is the best
estimate for f'(4)

00:32.454 --> 00:35.564
we can make based on this table?

00:35.564 --> 00:37.551
So, let's just visualize what's going on

00:37.551 --> 00:39.782
before we even look at the choices.

00:39.782 --> 00:42.192
So let me draw some axis here.

00:42.192 --> 00:44.062
And let me block these points.

00:44.062 --> 00:46.851
We know that Ds would
sit on the curve of Y

00:46.851 --> 00:48.564
is equal to f(x).

00:48.564 --> 00:50.573
When X is zero, f(x) is 72.

00:50.573 --> 00:52.533
So this is the .072.

00:52.533 --> 00:54.450
This is the .3 comma 95

00:55.539 --> 00:58.307
clearly, two different
scales on the X and Y axis.

00:58.307 --> 01:00.011
This is the point

01:00.011 --> 01:00.844
five 112.

01:01.904 --> 01:02.904
This is 677.

01:03.866 --> 01:05.808
This is 954, actually, let me

01:05.808 --> 01:06.831
write out

01:06.831 --> 01:07.664
the

01:08.607 --> 01:10.784
this is one, two, three,

01:10.784 --> 01:12.463
four, five, six,

01:12.463 --> 01:14.315
seven, eight, nine,

01:14.315 --> 01:15.148
and ten.

01:16.224 --> 01:18.141
Now they want us to know, they want to

01:18.141 --> 01:19.527
what is the derivative of our function

01:19.527 --> 01:20.400
when F is equal to four?

01:20.400 --> 01:21.403
Well, they haven't told us

01:21.403 --> 01:23.052
even what the value of F is at four.

01:23.052 --> 01:24.878
We don't know what that point is.

01:24.878 --> 01:26.166
But what they're trying to do is,

01:26.166 --> 01:27.838
well, we're trying to
make a best estimate.

01:27.838 --> 01:30.247
And using these points, we don't even know

01:30.247 --> 01:32.137
exactly what the curve looks like.

01:32.137 --> 01:33.820
It could look like all sorts of things.

01:33.820 --> 01:37.028
We could try to fit a
reasonably smooth curve.

01:37.028 --> 01:39.261
The curve might look something like that.

01:39.261 --> 01:40.906
But it might be wackier.

01:40.906 --> 01:44.145
It might do something like this.

01:44.145 --> 01:45.516
Let me try to do it.

01:45.516 --> 01:48.131
It might look something

01:48.131 --> 01:48.964
like this.

01:50.282 --> 01:51.724
So we don't know, for sure.

01:51.724 --> 01:52.975
All we know is that

01:52.975 --> 01:55.470
it needs to go through those points.

01:55.470 --> 01:57.570
'Cause they've just
sampled to the function

01:57.570 --> 01:58.994
at those points.

01:58.994 --> 02:00.573
But let's just, for the sake of

02:00.573 --> 02:02.856
this exercise, let's assume the simplest,

02:02.856 --> 02:04.387
let's say it's a nice smooth

02:04.387 --> 02:06.425
curve without too many twists and turns

02:06.425 --> 02:09.103
that goes through these
points, just like that.

02:09.103 --> 02:11.489
So what they're asking, okay,

02:11.489 --> 02:13.513
when X is equal to four

02:13.513 --> 02:17.027
if this yellow curve were the actual curve

02:17.027 --> 02:19.996
then what is the slope of the
tangent line, at that point?

02:19.996 --> 02:21.692
So we would be visualizing that.

02:21.692 --> 02:22.862
Now to be clear,

02:22.862 --> 02:24.654
this tangent line that I just drew

02:24.654 --> 02:25.568
this would be for

02:25.568 --> 02:27.589
this version of our function that I did

02:27.589 --> 02:28.721
connecting these points.

02:28.721 --> 02:30.627
That does not have to be

02:30.627 --> 02:31.677
the actual function.

02:31.677 --> 02:33.409
We know that the actual function has to go

02:33.409 --> 02:34.357
through those points.

02:34.357 --> 02:36.627
But I'm just doing this
for visualization purposes.

02:36.627 --> 02:39.069
One of the whole ideas here is

02:39.069 --> 02:40.917
that all we do have is the sample

02:40.917 --> 02:42.505
and we're trying to get a best estimate.

02:42.505 --> 02:43.387
We don't know

02:43.387 --> 02:44.983
if it's even gonna be a good estimate.

02:44.983 --> 02:47.178
It's just going to be a best estimate.

02:47.178 --> 02:48.664
So what we generally do

02:48.664 --> 02:50.606
when we just have some data

02:50.606 --> 02:52.861
around a point, is,
let's use a data points

02:52.861 --> 02:54.225
that are closest

02:54.225 --> 02:55.372
to that point

02:55.372 --> 02:58.061
and find slopes of secant lines

02:58.061 --> 03:00.046
pretty close around that point.

03:00.046 --> 03:02.543
And that's going to give
us our best estimate

03:02.543 --> 03:04.813
for the slope of the tangent line.

03:04.813 --> 03:07.048
So what points do we have near

03:07.048 --> 03:09.227
F of four, or near the point for

03:09.227 --> 03:10.772
comma F of four?

03:10.772 --> 03:13.151
Well, they give us what F is equal to

03:13.151 --> 03:14.543
when X is equal to three.

03:14.543 --> 03:16.153
They give us this point, right over here.

03:16.153 --> 03:18.027
Let me do this in another color.

03:18.027 --> 03:19.527
So, three comma 95

03:20.796 --> 03:22.775
that is that right over there.

03:22.775 --> 03:24.641
And they also give us

03:24.641 --> 03:26.717
five comma 112.

03:26.717 --> 03:28.680
That is that point right over there.

03:28.680 --> 03:30.128
And so what we could do

03:30.128 --> 03:31.681
we could say, well, what is the average

03:31.681 --> 03:33.643
rate of change between these two points?

03:33.643 --> 03:35.066
Another way to think about it is,

03:35.066 --> 03:37.309
what is the slope of the secant line

03:37.309 --> 03:38.898
between those two points.

03:38.898 --> 03:41.042
And, that would be our best estimate

03:41.042 --> 03:43.357
for the slope of the tangent line

03:43.357 --> 03:45.397
at X equals four.

03:45.397 --> 03:46.844
Do we know that it's a good estimate?

03:46.844 --> 03:48.187
Do we know that it's even close?

03:48.187 --> 03:49.577
No, we don't know for sure, but that would

03:49.577 --> 03:50.500
be the best estimate.

03:50.500 --> 03:52.573
It would be better than trying to take the

03:52.573 --> 03:54.372
the average rate of change between

03:54.372 --> 03:55.528
When X equals three

03:55.528 --> 03:56.405
and X equals six.

03:56.405 --> 03:58.113
Or between when X equals zero

03:58.113 --> 03:59.287
and X equals nine.

03:59.287 --> 04:01.835
These are pretty close around four.

04:01.835 --> 04:03.189
And so, let's do that.

04:03.189 --> 04:04.922
Let's find the average rate of change

04:04.922 --> 04:07.687
between when X goes from three to five.

04:07.687 --> 04:09.456
So, we can see here

04:09.456 --> 04:10.789
our change in X.

04:12.107 --> 04:13.749
Let me do this in a new color.

04:13.749 --> 04:15.658
So our change in X here

04:15.658 --> 04:17.163
is equal to

04:17.163 --> 04:18.037
plus two.

04:18.037 --> 04:19.682
And I can draw that out.

04:19.682 --> 04:21.454
My change in X here

04:21.454 --> 04:22.287
is

04:23.339 --> 04:24.956
plus two.

04:24.956 --> 04:27.021
And, my change in Y

04:27.021 --> 04:28.810
is going to be

04:28.810 --> 04:32.196
when my X increased by
two, my change in Y is

04:32.196 --> 04:34.278
